WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.960
Sometimes, seeing the trailers for upcoming
features can be the best part of going to

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:05.960
the theater.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.750
But as we all know, the final product can
be very different from the way it's portrayed

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.550
in the promo clips—in fact, sometimes, trailers
flat out lie to us.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.970
Here are some of the worst offenders.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.180
Snow Dogs

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.950
It sure looks like it has all the hallmarks
of a classic live-action talking animal movie:

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:27.119
it's made by Disney, shares a producer with
George of the Jungle and Charlotte's Web,

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:30.390
and the trailer even ends with a clip of wisecracking
dogs.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:35.289
But viewers expecting 2002's Snow Dogs to
be a raucous fish-out-of-water story with

00:00:35.289 --> 00:00:41.679
funny canines and copious pratfalls were given…
well, pretty much that, only without any talking

00:00:41.679 --> 00:00:43.100
animals.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.829
Anyone expecting Air Bud went home disappointed,
as the scene where the dogs mock Cuba Gooding

00:00:47.829 --> 00:00:50.700
Jr.'s character is actually from a dream sequence.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.100
"You really stepped in it this time!"

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.400
Kangaroo Jack

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.659
The blatantly misleading trailer for Snow
Dogs was a direct inspiration for Kangaroo

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:06.440
Jack's infamously misleading marketing, according
to the Los Angeles Times.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.300
Inspired by the earlier film, producer Jerry
Bruckheimer made Jack look less like a dark

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:16.120
mafia comedy and more like a light family
friendly romp focused on kangaroo-related

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.120
hijinx.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.800
"I said a hip, hop, beh hibbie, to the hip
hip hop and you don't stop the rock to the

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.800
bang bang boogie."

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:27.440
Like Snow Dogs, the talking animal scene is
just a dream, but the trailer doesn't make

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:28.550
that clear.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.450
A rapping kangaroo, combined with repeated
shots of the lead characters screaming and

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:38.370
pratfalling, helped to convince parents that
the movie was goofy family-friendly fare.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:44.270
Instead, thousands of kids found some surprisingly
lewd humor and a baffling plot about adult

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.409
friendships and failed dreams.

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:49.240
Sweeney Todd: The Demon Barber of Fleet Street

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:55.300
Yes, it's been musical on Broadway for decades,
but the trailer for Tim Burton's Sweeney Todd:

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.820
The Demon Barber of Fleet Street does everything
it can to hide that fact.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.890
A clip of Johnny Depp talk-singing is buried
within a preview that makes the gothic musical

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:10.409
look more like a combination of Dexter, The
Count of Monte Cristo, and an action thriller.

00:02:10.409 --> 00:02:14.689
Why the trailer does everything it can to
hide its musical roots is anyone's guess,

00:02:14.689 --> 00:02:18.959
especially when it looks identical to every
other Tim Burton movie without it.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:20.019
Observe and Report

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:26.739
An unrelenting, in-depth examination of a
profoundly disturbed individual, 2009's Observe

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:31.989
and Report is like Taxi Driver filtered through
the madcap antics of Seth Rogen's everyman

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:32.989
comedy stylings.

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:37.530
But you'd be hard-pressed to tell by watching
the trailer, which makes it look like a stealth

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.689
sequel to Paul Blart: Mall Cop—or at least
a rated-R parody of it.

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:46.459
The clip looks like it's advertising yet another
story of a schlubby guy in a crummy job finding

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:50.859
worth and acceptance in proving himself a
hero, but the actual plot of the movie is

00:02:50.859 --> 00:02:55.450
closer to the drug-filled last days of a genuine
psychopath.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:56.450
Godzilla

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:02.080
2014 was a mourning period for fans of Walter
White's misadventures on the popular TV show

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.060
Breaking Bad—it had been off for a year
by that point, and fans were missing Bryan

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.549
Cranston's nuanced portrayal of a beleaguered
family man making huge sacrifices for the

00:03:11.549 --> 00:03:13.049
ones he loves.

00:03:13.049 --> 00:03:17.719
But then the trailer for Godzilla arrived,
showcasing Cranston once again as a beleaguered

00:03:17.719 --> 00:03:21.709
family man, this time struggling to stop the
famous monster.

00:03:21.709 --> 00:03:26.409
Unfortunately, the trailer was clearly cut
once the marketing team realized how popular

00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:28.059
Cranston had become.

00:03:28.059 --> 00:03:33.909
He's only in the movie for just more than
a quarter of the runtime — a major bait-and-switch.

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:35.329
Ravenous

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:40.909
Going by the trailer, 1999's Ravenous looks
like a screwball comedy, or a Troma film that

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:42.860
was stuck in the vault for 20 years.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:47.370
What audiences missed out on was an audacious
horror-comedy, with a remarkable performance

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.890
by Guy Pearce.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:53.680
The movie's take on cannibalism deserves to
go down as a formative horror text, but it's

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.019
hard to convince anyone to watch after they've
seen the trailer.

00:03:57.019 --> 00:04:01.510
On the bright side, unlike many of the other
movies on this list, the only lie this trailer

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:05.109
is telling is that the movie isn't fantastic.

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:06.109
Drive

00:04:06.109 --> 00:04:11.829
Director Nicolas Refn's Drive is a slow, thorough
examination of crime and career criminals,

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:14.989
wrapped in layers of allegory and striking
cinematography.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:19.000
Which is why it's so ridiculous that the trailer
makes it look like a Fast and the Furious

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.290
flick, all pulse-pounding chase sequences
and fight scenes.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:26.920
The marketing was so misleading that one woman
even sued the distributor, complaining that

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.040
the movie had "very little driving."

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.990
All that's missing is Vin Diesel shouting
about "family."

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.750
"There's always room for family."

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:35.030
Thanks for watching!

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.600
